From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 08:50:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 141/190] cdc_hdr sat on an odd address, and the sector was
 always arriving

The emulator settles it. At the moment the transfer is triggered:

  DTTRG dac=C0F0 dbc=0803 pt=C0F0 buf=00232501 6038456D
  HOSTR#0 -> 0023   HOSTR#1 -> 2501   HOSTR#2 -> 6038 ...

DAC equals PT, the byte count is right, and the CDC hands over 00 23 25
01 -- the header for LBA 1600 -- followed by 60 38 45 6D, the first
bytes of our own boot sector. The disc, the drive, the seek, the
decoder, the ring and the transfer all work, and have been working.

cdc_hdr is a UBYTE[4]. Alignment 1, and the linker duly put it at an
odd address, where segacd_cdc_read() writes it with `move.w (a2),(a1)+`.
The emulator's 68000 masks the low bit rather than faulting, so both
header words land one byte low and the array reads back 23 25 01 00.
The header check then rejects a perfectly good sector as the wrong one:
cd_why 0x5555, four attempts, every read, for as long as there has been
a header check.

On the console it would not have been a wrong answer, it would have
been an address error -- and the only reason it never fired is that no
transfer has ever completed there.

cdd_rawbuf and cdd_outbuf in this same file already carry this fix, on
the line that declares them, and say why. So does progs/cdtest.c, whose
OSBUF comment describes finding it the same way after two panics.
Third time in one file.

With four bytes moved: cd_why 0, wrong sectors 0, errors 1 instead of
3, cdc_hdr 00 23 25 01, and cd_dtest bit 1 set for the first time --
"a block came out of the disc and went to GEMDOS". The status line
reads DY.
---
 bios/scdapi.h |  2 +-
 bios/segacd.c | 25 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 25 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/scdapi.h b/bios/scdapi.h
index 471152f..1abab03 100644
--- a/bios/scdapi.h
+++ b/bios/scdapi.h
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@
 #define SCDAPI_H
 
 #define SCD_COOKIE   0x53674344L        /* 'SgCD' */
-#define SCD_VERSION  9
+#define SCD_VERSION  10
 
 /* Everything the disc diagnostic prints, in one go. Taken in a single
  * call rather than twenty accessors so that the numbers in one report
diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index f626036..527f502 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -1187,7 +1187,30 @@ static volatile UBYTE cdc_hit;  /* it went by, and cdc_pt says where */
 static volatile UBYTE cdc_hit_age;      /* cdc_ints when that happened, so
                                          * the staleness test that guards a
                                          * ring entry can guard this too */
-static UBYTE cdc_hdr[4];        /* header that arrived with the data */
+/* Aligned, because segacd_cdc_read() writes it with `move.w (a2),(a1)+`.
+ *
+ * A UBYTE[4] has alignment 1 and gcc is right to give it that; the
+ * linker put this one at an odd address and has for as long as the
+ * driver has existed. Two things follow, and both were in front of us.
+ *
+ * In the emulator, whose 68000 masks the low address bit rather than
+ * faulting, the two header words land one byte low: the CDC hands over
+ * 00 23 25 01 -- the correct header for LBA 1600, with the correct
+ * sector behind it -- and this array reads back 23 25 01 00. The header
+ * check then calls a perfectly good sector the wrong sector, which is
+ * why every attempt of every read scored why nibble 5.
+ *
+ * On the console it is worse: `move.w` to an odd address is an address
+ * error, so the first transfer that ever completed would take the
+ * machine down. None has, because the drive has never delivered one.
+ * This has been waiting behind the drive problem the whole time.
+ *
+ * cdd_rawbuf and cdd_outbuf above already carry this fix and say so on
+ * the line that declares them. So does progs/cdtest.c, whose OSBUF
+ * comment describes the identical bug found the identical way. Third
+ * time, same file. */
+static ULONG cdc_hdrbuf[1];
+#define cdc_hdr ((UBYTE *)cdc_hdrbuf)   /* header that arrived with the data */
 static UBYTE cdc_fail;          /* why the read did not finish */
 static UWORD cdc_spill;         /* words drained while waiting for the end
                                  * of a transfer; kept in a variable so the
